{"gid":"2026_01_ARI_LAC","week":1,"away":"ARI","home":"LAC","away_col":"#E64868","home_col":"#3AC7F0","spread":11.5,"total":45.5,"fav":"LAC","hpts":28,"apts":17,"kickoff":"Sun Sep 13 \u00b7 16:25","roof":"Dome","report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><p><strong>Ground mismatch:</strong> LAC are run-first (40% pass when leading) into a ARI front that bleeds <strong>128 rush yds/game</strong> and 0.47 RB1 rushing scores. <strong>Omarion Hampton</strong> is a centerpiece \u2014 and the game script only feeds him.</p><p>The <strong>LAC</strong> host the <strong>ARI</strong> as favorites of <strong>11.5</strong>, with the total set at <strong>45.5</strong> \u2014 a market that implies a <strong>LAC 28\u201317</strong> scoreline. Running the game forward from that baseline, we expect a contest that spends roughly <strong>56% of its snaps with LAC in front</strong>, <strong>26% within one score</strong>, and <strong>18% with LAC chasing</strong>. That distribution is the engine of everything below: game state is what turns a team's tendencies into a specific player's workload, so before we get to individuals it's worth being explicit about <em>how</em> this game is likely to be played.</p><p>This projects as a game <strong>LAC should control</strong>. A favorite of 12 sits in front for the majority of the second half in most simulations, which pulls the script toward the run and clock management for <strong>LAC</strong> and toward volume passing and tempo for <strong>ARI</strong>, who will be throwing to keep pace. Expect <strong>ARI</strong> to run more total plays than LAC \u2014 trailing teams go no-huddle (league no-huddle rate climbs from ~8% in neutral game states to over 20% when down two scores), and that extra volume is a tailwind for ARI's pass-catchers even in a losing effort.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Oronde Gadsden II</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 ARI are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.32 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p><strong>Ladd McConkey</strong> carries TD equity \u2014 ARI surrender 0.47 WR1 receiving scores a game.</p><p>In the red zone ARI throw it (59% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Michael Wilson</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p><strong>Script leans LAC.</strong> As a 12-point favorite they project to lead much of the game, tilting toward the run late \u2014 <strong>Omarion Hampton</strong>'s carry ceiling rises in the blowout branches. Meanwhile ARI throw to keep pace, a volume tailwind for <strong>Michael Wilson</strong>.</p><p>Fade the ARI run \u2014 as 12-point dogs projected to chase, <strong>James Conner</strong>'s carries get squeezed, though his receiving work ticks up in catch-up mode.</p><p>A middling 46 total \u2014 no strong lean either way; the value is in the individual matchups above, not the game environment.</p><p><strong>Indoors</strong> \u2014 a dome removes weather variance and historically nudges passing efficiency up; a small tailwind for the passing games.</p><h3>LAC \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>LAC are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>40%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>This is the profile that most rewards a lead back. As a 12-point favorite projected to lead for 56% of snaps, <strong>LAC</strong> will be running to close the game, and their run-first identity compounds it. <strong>Omarion Hampton</strong> is the beneficiary \u2014 we project <strong>10 carries for 43 yards</strong>, and his ceiling is where the value sits: in the simulations where this becomes a two-score game, his volume spikes as LAC salt it away.</p><p>The flip side: <strong>Ladd McConkey</strong> (5 targets, 42 yards) has his ceiling <em>capped</em> by the same script that helps the run game \u2014 when LAC lead and run, there simply aren't as many pass attempts to go around. He's a safer floor than ceiling play in this spot.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Oronde Gadsden II</strong> (TE, 3 targets, 29 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>ARI \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>ARI are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (45% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 ARI project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>James Conner</strong>'s rushing projection (11 carries, 46 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Michael Wilson</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, ARI funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>9 targets for 78 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Trey McBride</strong> (TE, 10 targets, 74 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 12-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"ARI","col":"#E64868","players":[{"name":"Jacoby Brissett","pos":"QB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":225.4,"book":222.0,"over_th":210.69,"under_th":235.25,"over_am":"\u2212115","under_am":"\u2212114","call":"none","ev":-6.0,"edge":3.4},{"label":"Pass TDs","proj":1.24,"book":1.5,"over_th":0.97,"under_th":1.36,"over_am":"+163","under_am":"\u2212222","call":"OVER","ev":8.8,"edge":-0.3}],"td":{"pct":10.9,"fair":"9/1","book":"9/1","value":true}},{"name":"Jeremiyah Love","pos":"RB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Rush yards","proj":42.92,"book":null,"over_th":33.59,"under_th":42.35,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null},{"label":"Carries","proj":10.01,"book":null,"over_th":8.68,"under_th":10.27,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null},{"label":"Rec yards","proj":13.65,"book":null,"over_th":7.6,"under_th":11.68,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null},{"label":"Receptions","proj":1.89,"book":null,"over_th":1.17,"under_th":1.42,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null}],"td":{"pct":26.5,"fair":"3/1","book":"7/4","value":false}},{"name":"Tyler Allgeier","pos":"RB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Rush yards","proj":24.73,"book":null,"over_th":16.14,"under_th":23.37,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null},{"label":"Carries","proj":5.72,"book":null,"over_th":4.4,"under_th":5.58,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null},{"label":"Rec yards","proj":13.85,"book":null,"over_th":7.79,"under_th":11.84,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null},{"label":"Recepti